1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:00,950
You wake up,

2
00:00:01,270 --> 00:00:01,870
look in the mirror,

3
00:00:02,290 --> 00:00:04,330
and there's a number floating above your head.

4
00:00:04,330 --> 00:00:07,480
Forty-one thousand, two hundred dollars.

5
00:00:07,480 --> 00:00:09,410
Your exact income this year.

6
00:00:10,010 --> 00:00:11,450
Every dollar, from every source.

7
00:00:12,410 --> 00:00:13,170
You look out the window.

8
00:00:13,970 --> 00:00:15,010
Everyone has one.

9
00:00:15,630 --> 00:00:16,530
For the next seven days,

10
00:00:16,850 --> 00:00:18,870
you can see what every single person earns.

11
00:00:19,570 --> 00:00:20,590
And by day seven,

12
00:00:20,990 --> 00:00:23,150
one of those numbers is going to ruin your life.

13
00:00:23,890 --> 00:00:25,910
At first, it's just funny.

14
00:00:26,510 --> 00:00:28,050
The guy in the expensive suit earns

15
00:00:28,050 --> 00:00:29,430
barely more than the barista.

16
00:00:29,920 --> 00:00:31,890
The lady in sweatpants walking her dog?

17
00:00:31,890 --> 00:00:33,270
Over a million.

18
00:00:34,010 --> 00:00:36,250
Lesson one, before you've even had breakfast:

19
00:00:36,790 --> 00:00:39,450
you cannot tell what people earn by looking at them.

20
00:00:40,290 --> 00:00:41,150
It gets stranger.

21
00:00:41,910 --> 00:00:43,550
The kid on the skateboard makes more

22
00:00:43,550 --> 00:00:45,090
than your whole office floor.

23
00:00:45,710 --> 00:00:47,890
He posts videos about skateboarding.

24
00:00:48,330 --> 00:00:50,250
The nurse who just worked twelve hours

25
00:00:50,250 --> 00:00:52,450
saving lives makes a fifth of that.

26
00:00:53,210 --> 00:00:55,030
You stand on the corner for a long time,

27
00:00:55,390 --> 00:00:56,990
just watching the numbers go by.

28
00:00:57,590 --> 00:00:58,610
Because for the first time,

29
00:00:59,050 --> 00:01:01,390
you're seeing something nobody is supposed to see:

30
00:01:01,870 --> 00:01:03,870
what the world actually pays for.

31
00:01:04,630 --> 00:01:05,910
Then you walk into work.

32
00:01:06,930 --> 00:01:07,110
Kevin.

33
00:01:07,690 --> 00:01:10,570
Kevin who forwards your emails and calls it teamwork.

34
00:01:11,330 --> 00:01:12,877
Kevin makes almost seventeen

35
00:01:12,877 --> 00:01:14,970
thousand dollars more than you.

36
00:01:15,610 --> 00:01:16,870
And here's the uncomfortable part.

37
00:01:17,510 --> 00:01:18,210
In most offices,

38
00:01:18,430 --> 00:01:19,510
nobody talks about pay,

39
00:01:19,870 --> 00:01:21,690
so gaps like this can hide for years.

40
00:01:22,430 --> 00:01:24,210
That's exactly why a growing number of places

41
00:01:24,210 --> 00:01:26,530
now require salary ranges in job ads.

42
00:01:26,530 --> 00:01:28,050
Several U.S. states do it,

43
00:01:28,050 --> 00:01:30,430
and the European Union passed a pay transparency

44
00:01:30,430 --> 00:01:32,890
law that pushes companies to explain pay gaps.

45
00:01:33,330 --> 00:01:35,610
The secret salary is slowly dying.

46
00:01:36,170 --> 00:01:37,050
You're just seeing it early.

47
00:01:38,010 --> 00:01:39,830
So you do something you've never done before.

48
00:01:40,450 --> 00:01:41,670
You ask for a raise.

49
00:01:42,450 --> 00:01:43,050
And this time,

50
00:01:43,450 --> 00:01:44,670
you have an unfair advantage.

51
00:01:45,250 --> 00:01:46,370
You can see the budget.

52
00:01:46,970 --> 00:01:48,070
You know what the role is worth.

53
00:01:48,550 --> 00:01:48,950
You don't guess.

54
00:01:49,430 --> 00:01:50,150
You don't apologize.

55
00:01:50,570 --> 00:01:51,290
You say a number.

56
00:01:51,290 --> 00:01:53,830
"…Fifty-five?"

57
00:01:53,830 --> 00:01:55,310
Negotiation researchers have found that

58
00:01:55,310 --> 00:01:57,330
many people never ask for more at all,

59
00:01:57,810 --> 00:01:58,650
and over a whole career,

60
00:01:59,010 --> 00:02:00,510
that silence can cost a fortune.

61
00:02:01,290 --> 00:02:03,070
You didn't need superpowers to ask.

62
00:02:03,630 --> 00:02:05,750
You just needed to know you were allowed to.

63
00:02:06,710 --> 00:02:09,130
After work, your best friend Leo picks you up.

64
00:02:09,530 --> 00:02:10,630
Leo has the new car.

65
00:02:11,070 --> 00:02:11,590
The watch.

66
00:02:11,770 --> 00:02:12,430
The weekend trips.

67
00:02:12,670 --> 00:02:13,150
The photos.

68
00:02:13,470 --> 00:02:16,390
And a number barely higher than yours.

69
00:02:17,030 --> 00:02:18,590
For years you've felt like you

70
00:02:18,590 --> 00:02:19,810
were falling behind Leo.

71
00:02:20,490 --> 00:02:22,850
Now you know Leo is falling too.

72
00:02:23,270 --> 00:02:24,130
Just more quietly.

73
00:02:24,990 --> 00:02:26,910
Researchers studying lottery winners in

74
00:02:26,910 --> 00:02:28,990
Canada found something wild about this.

75
00:02:29,370 --> 00:02:31,370
The bigger a neighbour's lottery win,

76
00:02:31,750 --> 00:02:33,830
the more the people living next door borrowed,

77
00:02:33,830 --> 00:02:35,450
and the more of them went bankrupt.

78
00:02:35,850 --> 00:02:37,490
Not because they won anything.

79
00:02:37,930 --> 00:02:39,170
Because they tried to keep up.

80
00:02:39,530 --> 00:02:40,590
You decide two things.

81
00:02:40,990 --> 00:02:43,730
You'll stop comparing yourself to Leo's photos.

82
00:02:44,230 --> 00:02:45,650
And you'll ask him if he's okay.

83
00:02:45,650 --> 00:02:47,470
That night, you have a date.

84
00:02:48,150 --> 00:02:50,510
Maya earns more than three times what you do.

85
00:02:50,870 --> 00:02:51,650
And for a second,

86
00:02:51,670 --> 00:02:54,170
something ugly crawls into your chest.

87
00:02:54,970 --> 00:02:56,590
Psychologists have a name for this.

88
00:02:56,970 --> 00:02:57,790
Social comparison.

89
00:02:58,370 --> 00:03:00,430
We don't judge our lives by how much we have.

90
00:03:00,430 --> 00:03:02,310
We judge them by how much we have

91
00:03:02,310 --> 00:03:04,590
compared to the people around us.

92
00:03:04,990 --> 00:03:05,850
Maya didn't change.

93
00:03:06,390 --> 00:03:07,650
Her number just appeared.

94
00:03:08,370 --> 00:03:10,730
You decide not to let a number sit at the table.

95
00:03:11,430 --> 00:03:12,430
She pays for dessert.

96
00:03:12,950 --> 00:03:13,530
You let her.

97
00:03:14,030 --> 00:03:14,950
On the walk to the station,

98
00:03:15,150 --> 00:03:16,390
Maya tells you she grew up sharing

99
00:03:16,390 --> 00:03:18,030
one bedroom with three sisters.

100
00:03:18,510 --> 00:03:20,010
That big number above her head?

101
00:03:20,270 --> 00:03:22,570
She built it one late night at a time.

102
00:03:23,110 --> 00:03:24,930
You realize you were jealous of the number.

103
00:03:25,450 --> 00:03:27,430
You never once thought about the work behind it.

104
00:03:27,910 --> 00:03:30,230
Walking home, you ask yourself the big question.

105
00:03:30,430 --> 00:03:32,850
Would you be happier with her number?

106
00:03:32,850 --> 00:03:35,430
Scientists have argued about this for years.

107
00:03:36,010 --> 00:03:37,770
One famous study suggested happiness

108
00:03:37,770 --> 00:03:39,850
stops rising above a certain income.

109
00:03:40,310 --> 00:03:42,190
Newer research found that for most people,

110
00:03:42,190 --> 00:03:44,330
happiness keeps rising as income grows,

111
00:03:44,330 --> 00:03:45,770
just more slowly.

112
00:03:46,430 --> 00:03:49,030
Each extra dollar matters less than the one before.

113
00:03:49,690 --> 00:03:50,410
More money helps.

114
00:03:50,750 --> 00:03:52,350
But the climb gets steeper,

115
00:03:52,350 --> 00:03:54,510
and the view changes less than you'd expect.

116
00:03:55,510 --> 00:03:56,570
On day five,

117
00:03:56,810 --> 00:03:57,630
you visit your parents.

118
00:03:58,370 --> 00:04:00,490
Your dad's number is lower than yours.

119
00:04:00,910 --> 00:04:03,970
Lower than yours was before the raise.

120
00:04:03,970 --> 00:04:04,470
The bike.

121
00:04:04,910 --> 00:04:05,710
The school trips.

122
00:04:06,190 --> 00:04:07,670
The laptop for college.

123
00:04:08,190 --> 00:04:08,750
All of it,

124
00:04:08,750 --> 00:04:09,650
from that number.

125
00:04:10,570 --> 00:04:12,990
You realize a salary tells you what someone earns.

126
00:04:12,990 --> 00:04:15,410
It tells you nothing about what they've given.

127
00:04:16,110 --> 00:04:18,790
Later, you ask your dad if he ever wanted more money.

128
00:04:18,790 --> 00:04:20,250
He laughs.

129
00:04:20,250 --> 00:04:21,010
"Of course I did.

130
00:04:21,470 --> 00:04:23,430
But I wanted to be home for dinner more."

131
00:04:24,210 --> 00:04:25,870
After dinner, you find your mum doing

132
00:04:25,870 --> 00:04:27,170
the bills at the kitchen table.

133
00:04:27,830 --> 00:04:28,970
Every column balanced.

134
00:04:29,390 --> 00:04:30,270
Every bill paid.

135
00:04:30,270 --> 00:04:31,810
For thirty years.

136
00:04:32,410 --> 00:04:33,570
She's never earned much.

137
00:04:33,890 --> 00:04:36,290
But nobody in this house has ever gone without.

138
00:04:36,970 --> 00:04:38,110
You sit down next to her,

139
00:04:38,110 --> 00:04:39,070
and for once,

140
00:04:39,070 --> 00:04:41,290
you ask her to teach you how she does it.

141
00:04:42,030 --> 00:04:43,630
Then, on day six,

142
00:04:44,110 --> 00:04:44,890
something changes.

143
00:04:45,470 --> 00:04:46,730
It isn't just you anymore.

144
00:04:47,350 --> 00:04:49,190
Everyone can see everyone's number.

145
00:04:49,650 --> 00:04:51,610
And the world goes a little crazy.

146
00:04:52,430 --> 00:04:53,890
Landlords start raising rent

147
00:04:53,890 --> 00:04:55,110
for tenants with big numbers.

148
00:04:55,810 --> 00:04:58,270
Charities only knock on the richest doors.

149
00:04:58,270 --> 00:04:59,330
And for the first time,

150
00:04:59,450 --> 00:05:01,890
every boss has to walk past employees who

151
00:05:01,890 --> 00:05:04,130
can see exactly how big the gap is.

152
00:05:04,690 --> 00:05:05,450
At your office,

153
00:05:05,530 --> 00:05:07,470
there's a line outside HR that

154
00:05:07,470 --> 00:05:08,450
wraps around the building.

155
00:05:08,870 --> 00:05:10,150
Even Kevin is in it.

156
00:05:10,150 --> 00:05:12,690
You finally ask him how he makes more than you.

157
00:05:13,110 --> 00:05:14,010
He looks embarrassed.

158
00:05:14,790 --> 00:05:15,850
"When they offered me the job,

159
00:05:15,850 --> 00:05:17,270
I just asked for more.

160
00:05:17,510 --> 00:05:19,210
They said yes."

161
00:05:19,210 --> 00:05:19,630
That's it.

162
00:05:20,010 --> 00:05:21,650
One sentence, years ago.

163
00:05:22,330 --> 00:05:23,430
That's the thing about salaries.

164
00:05:23,430 --> 00:05:26,790
A small difference at the start follows you for years,

165
00:05:27,170 --> 00:05:28,950
because every raise is built on top

166
00:05:28,950 --> 00:05:30,350
of the number you began with.

167
00:05:31,050 --> 00:05:32,070
Here's the wild part.

168
00:05:32,350 --> 00:05:33,690
This has actually happened.

169
00:05:34,250 --> 00:05:36,770
In Norway, everyone's income and taxes have

170
00:05:36,770 --> 00:05:38,510
been public records for a long time.

171
00:05:38,770 --> 00:05:39,910
And in 2001,

172
00:05:40,030 --> 00:05:41,410
those records went online,

173
00:05:41,570 --> 00:05:42,890
searchable by anyone,

174
00:05:42,890 --> 00:05:43,850
in seconds.

175
00:05:44,570 --> 00:05:46,310
Economists studied what happened next.

176
00:05:46,950 --> 00:05:48,150
When everyone could suddenly look

177
00:05:48,150 --> 00:05:49,390
up everyone else's income,

178
00:05:49,890 --> 00:05:51,370
the happiness gap between richer

179
00:05:51,370 --> 00:05:52,610
and poorer people grew.

180
00:05:53,370 --> 00:05:54,470
Seeing other people's numbers

181
00:05:54,470 --> 00:05:55,650
didn't make people richer.

182
00:05:56,210 --> 00:05:56,930
For a lot of them,

183
00:05:56,990 --> 00:05:58,450
it just made them feel poorer.

184
00:05:59,370 --> 00:06:02,030
Years later, Norway made searches traceable,

185
00:06:02,170 --> 00:06:04,150
so people could see who had looked them up.

186
00:06:04,650 --> 00:06:06,370
Searches fell from around sixteen

187
00:06:06,370 --> 00:06:08,490
million a year to about two million.

188
00:06:09,510 --> 00:06:11,170
Remember the number from the first morning?

189
00:06:11,170 --> 00:06:13,330
The one that was going to ruin your life?

190
00:06:13,330 --> 00:06:14,790
On day seven,

191
00:06:14,790 --> 00:06:16,170
you finally see it.

192
00:06:16,470 --> 00:06:18,670
It's above the quiet janitor in your building.

193
00:06:18,870 --> 00:06:20,710
The one who's worked there for thirty years.

194
00:06:20,710 --> 00:06:23,070
Nine point eight million.

195
00:06:23,730 --> 00:06:26,090
He bought the building's shares in the eighties.

196
00:06:26,090 --> 00:06:26,850
Saved every month.

197
00:06:27,210 --> 00:06:27,950
Never told anyone.

198
00:06:28,630 --> 00:06:30,810
Today, the dividends alone pay him more

199
00:06:30,810 --> 00:06:32,470
than everyone in the office combined.

200
00:06:33,190 --> 00:06:34,010
He waves at you.

201
00:06:34,130 --> 00:06:34,870
Same as always.

202
00:06:35,510 --> 00:06:37,430
At lunch, you sit next to him on the loading dock.

203
00:06:37,430 --> 00:06:39,430
You ask the only question that matters.

204
00:06:40,130 --> 00:06:41,290
Why are you still here?

205
00:06:41,950 --> 00:06:42,290
He shrugs.

206
00:06:43,110 --> 00:06:43,650
"I like the work.

207
00:06:43,970 --> 00:06:44,670
I like the people.

208
00:06:44,830 --> 00:06:46,970
And I never needed anyone to know my number."

209
00:06:47,730 --> 00:06:49,930
You ask him if he ever felt like giving up on saving.

210
00:06:50,890 --> 00:06:52,470
"Every month for thirty years,

211
00:06:52,570 --> 00:06:53,590
I put a little away.

212
00:06:54,050 --> 00:06:55,270
Most months, it felt like nothing.

213
00:06:55,690 --> 00:06:56,250
Then one day,

214
00:06:56,250 --> 00:06:57,030
I looked up,

215
00:06:57,030 --> 00:06:57,770
and it was everything."

216
00:06:58,470 --> 00:06:59,990
Then he gives you the only financial

217
00:06:59,990 --> 00:07:01,370
advice he's ever given anyone.

218
00:07:02,030 --> 00:07:03,150
"Spend less than you make.

219
00:07:03,150 --> 00:07:04,890
Own things that pay you.

220
00:07:04,890 --> 00:07:06,930
And stop looking at other people's numbers."

221
00:07:07,430 --> 00:07:08,770
It didn't ruin your life.

222
00:07:09,150 --> 00:07:11,270
It ruined the way you used to judge people.

223
00:07:11,270 --> 00:07:13,310
At midnight, the numbers fade.

224
00:07:13,790 --> 00:07:15,250
The last one to go is your own.

225
00:07:15,690 --> 00:07:16,170
A week ago,

226
00:07:16,290 --> 00:07:17,410
that number felt like a grade.

227
00:07:17,730 --> 00:07:19,690
Like a score of how much you were worth.

228
00:07:20,190 --> 00:07:21,550
Now it just looks like what it is.

229
00:07:21,990 --> 00:07:24,950
A price someone agreed to pay for your time this year.

230
00:07:25,350 --> 00:07:26,910
It says nothing about your dad's cooking,

231
00:07:27,250 --> 00:07:28,030
your mum's notebook,

232
00:07:28,170 --> 00:07:30,170
or the janitor who waves every morning.

233
00:07:30,750 --> 00:07:32,350
Here's what seven days of seeing

234
00:07:32,350 --> 00:07:33,930
everyone's salary taught you.

235
00:07:34,210 --> 00:07:35,790
You can't read wealth from clothes.

236
00:07:35,790 --> 00:07:38,830
Not knowing what others earn can cost you real money,

237
00:07:38,910 --> 00:07:39,650
so ask.

238
00:07:40,290 --> 00:07:41,850
Comparing numbers can steal your

239
00:07:41,850 --> 00:07:43,550
happiness faster than any bill.

240
00:07:44,170 --> 00:07:45,830
And the number above someone's head is

241
00:07:45,830 --> 00:07:47,510
the least interesting thing about them.

242
00:07:47,990 --> 00:07:50,070
Tomorrow, you wake up and see a different number.

243
00:07:50,530 --> 00:07:50,930
Not money.

244
00:07:51,650 --> 00:07:51,910
Karma.

245
00:07:52,910 --> 00:07:55,150
If you want to see what happens next,

246
00:07:55,430 --> 00:07:55,950
subscribe.

247
00:07:56,850 --> 00:07:57,850
Because tomorrow at midnight,

248
00:07:57,850 --> 00:07:59,330
the numbers change again.
